Janis Papanagnou wrote:

 > My observation is that you are very dependent on the environment
 > you have in your actual game.

That's why I advocate adjusting to it as good as you can.

 > Early shops (for example), or an altar can help a more difficult
 > role to survive easier. An "empty" dungeon can evolve the game of
 > an easier role to a nightmare;

I think we all agree that every role can benefit from early shops and
altars, or useful items generated randomly on the floor. And that the
absence of those similarly makes it harder for all roles as well.

But shops, altars and items have nothing to do with role difficulty
per se and thus don't matter.

If you want to define role difficulty, you can only do it by looking
at the advantages and disadvantages the roles themselves offer. And
once you do that, you will find that the differences that matter can
be overcome with knowledge, experience, and proper strategy.

 > One effect of specific roles is that they have some more, even
 > crucial options available; dipping for Excalibur has been
 > mentioned.

*Alignment* advantage, not role advantage.

 > Why are there so many Wizard start-scummers? I think it's because
 > of their varying inventory of different usefulness; some items and
 > item combinations are very good others are just useless crap.

People start-scum, because they think they can increase their chances
to win that way. I used to think so, too. I start-scummed for a ring
of slow disgestion, because I had food problems. I start-scummed for a
wand of polymorph to upgrade my pet. I start-scummed for magic missile
as second spell so I could kill monsters easier.

And you know what? It didn't help, I died anyway #-). Because I didn't
know how to play them properly.

Now that I do, I can ascend several wizards in a row, regardless of
their starting equipment. If there's something useful, I use it, if
not, who cares, it is not required to win anyway. The only exception
(as mentioned in another post) is a magic marker, since that turns a
game into nearly-guaranteed ascension.

 > As a non-beginner I'd say, role matters a lot for beginners, still
 > for experienced players. But actual "environment" matters more,
 > especially for non-beginners.

My point is that "role difficulty" is a meaningless qualifier.

Just consider valkyries. One variaty (Law Dwa) can dip for Excalibur,
has peaceful mines, and infravision. The other (Neu Hum) cannot dip
for Excalibur, has hostile mines, no infravision, and only gets an
artifact weapon after finding and possibly converting an altar (which
can mean getting the scrolls of earth from Sokoban and converting the
Minetown temple with all its consequences). All valkyries are easy?

Tourists for example aren't difficult because they are tourists. They
are difficult because they are humans. (Yes, I know that tourists can
only be humans.) Think about it. Consider a theoretical "Gno Tou" with
infravision who kills monsters by throwing his +2 darts from afar and
who enjoys peaceful mines. Still that difficult?

"Difficulty" is comprised of role, race, alignment, and environment.
Role alone is meaningless for judging difficulty.

        /stth



